WEBVTT

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.340
What if one of history's most powerful pirates

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:04.800
was not a man, but a woman commanding

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:05.855
hundreds of ships?

00:00:06.095 --> 00:00:09.240
In 1807, Zheng Yi Sao assumed control of

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.540
a massive pirate fleet within the South China

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:11.985
Sea.

00:00:12.225 --> 00:00:15.970
The Chinese emperor dispatched imperial warships to dismantle

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:17.520
her growing maritime power.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:21.685
Yet, her forces proved resilient, successfully defeating multiple

00:00:21.685 --> 00:00:23.960
imperial fleets in open combat.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.525
For years, her alliance terrorized merchant vessels and

00:00:27.525 --> 00:00:29.410
coastal towns across the region.

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:34.390
Eventually, government pressure intensified while internal rivalries began

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:36.310
to weaken her pirate coalition.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:39.695
In 1810, Zheng Yi Sao achieved the extraordinary

00:00:39.695 --> 00:00:41.910
by negotiating a formal pardon.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:44.955
She retired with her wealth intact, escaping the

00:00:44.955 --> 00:00:47.795
violent fate often met by famous pirates like

00:00:47.795 --> 00:00:48.475
Blackbeard.

00:00:48.715 --> 00:00:52.900
She remained free and lived until 1844, defying

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.405
the typical end for such a notorious figure.
